const d={"the-old-hall-ely":[2e3,1125],"couple-dancing":[2048,1370],"guests-dancing":[2048,1365],"band-and-dance-floor":[2048,1365],"five-piece-band":[2048,1365],"dance-floor-moves":[1e3,648]},o=(a,i,h)=>({type:"image",src:`/images/blog/jen-and-tom/${a}.webp`,alt:i,caption:h,width:(d[a]||[1e3,667])[0],height:(d[a]||[1e3,667])[1]}),u={slug:"jen-and-toms-wedding",title:"Jen & Tom’s Wedding at The Old Hall Ely",metaTitle:"Jen & Tom’s Wedding at The Old Hall Ely | The Supreme Collective",metaDescription:"Revisit Jen and Tom’s April 2017 wedding at The Old Hall Ely in Cambridgeshire, with a five-piece party band and photographs from their celebration.",excerpt:"A five-piece party band, a star-lit marquee and a Cambridgeshire celebration: revisit Jen and Tom’s wedding at The Old Hall Ely.",category:"Real Weddings",author:"Rhona Downie",authorType:"Person",publishedAt:"2017-04-23",readTime:"3 min read",heroImage:"/images/blog/jen-and-tom/first-dance.webp",heroImageAlt:"The bride and groom sharing their first dance on a chequered floor, with the five-piece band onstage behind them",heroImageFit:"contain",heroImageWidth:2048,heroImageHeight:1365,content:[{type:"paragraph",text:"On 1 April 2017, we sent our five-piece party band—two vocalists, guitar, bass and drums—to The Old Hall Ely in Cambridgeshire. And firstly, may I say, don’t hold the words “old hall” against this venue. There was no musty old town hall in sight; instead, the band rolled up to this gorgeous, picturesque venue."},o("the-old-hall-ely","The Old Hall Ely’s brick manor house and landscaped gardens, with Ely Cathedral visible in the distance","The Old Hall Ely, Cambridgeshire."),{type:"paragraph",text:"The band members arrived at 5pm, giving them 90 minutes for setup and soundcheck. They began setting up in the marquee, which was permanently attached to the venue. The marquee already had a black star-lit effect on the walls and ceiling, and led straight to the dining area."},{type:"paragraph",text:"They worked quickly and quietly, since the wedding guests were just on the other side of the door, while waiting for the green light from Alex Moresby, wedding planner and stylist extraordinaire, to fit soundcheck around the speeches as requested by Jen."},{type:"paragraph",text:"After soundcheck, the band grabbed a meal from the BBQ to fuel up. They also posed for a photograph with our official Supreme Collective photographer, Natalja Safronova, whom we had brought along before the doors opened and the party began."},o("five-piece-band","Two vocalists, a guitarist, a bassist and a drummer posing onstage beneath coloured lights and a star-lit backdrop","The five-piece band before the party."),{type:"heading",text:"Photographs from the celebration"},o("guests-mingling","Wedding guests chatting at tables with white flowers beneath the marquee’s warm fairy lights"),o("wedding-guests","The groom and three wedding guests posing together beside the reception tables"),o("couple-dancing","The bride and groom dancing together among their guests beneath pink party lighting"),o("band-and-dance-floor","Wedding guests filling the chequered dance floor while the live band performs beneath a star-lit backdrop"),o("dance-floor-moves","Two wedding guests showing their dance moves as friends cheer around the chequered floor"),o("guests-with-band","The groom dancing in the middle of a circle of wedding guests beside the live band"),o("party-dancing","Two wedding guests dancing beneath the marquee’s star-lit ceiling"),o("guests-dancing","Wedding guests laughing and dancing together under colourful lights"),o("dancing-close-up","A wedding guest dancing in front of the band’s microphones and drum kit"),{type:"paragraph",text:"Wedding-day photography: Natalja Safronova. These photographs and the account above are from the 2017 celebration; venue layouts and arrangements may have changed since then."},{type:"heading",text:"Planning your own Cambridgeshire wedding?"},{type:"paragraph",text:"Thinking about the look of your evening party? Our wedding band lighting guide explains what to ask about stage lights, dance-floor effects and the lighting your venue already provides.",links:[{text:"wedding band lighting guide",href:"/blog/essential-stage-lighting-for-your-wedding-band/"}]},{type:"paragraph",text:"Explore our wedding bands in Cambridgeshire, browse the venue guide, or send us your date and venue to talk through the music for your celebration.",links:[{text:"wedding bands in Cambridgeshire",href:"/wedding-bands-cambridgeshire/"},{text:"venue guide",href:"/venues/?county=Cambridgeshire"},{text:"send us your date and venue",href:"/acts/"}]},{type:"paragraph",text:"For current venue information, visit The Old Hall Ely.",links:[{text:"The Old Hall Ely",href:"https://theoldhallely.co.uk/"}]}]},p={slug:"essential-stage-lighting-for-your-wedding-band",title:"Essential Stage Lighting for Your Wedding Band",metaTitle:"Wedding Band Lighting: What to Ask Before Booking | The Supreme Collective",metaDescription:"Plan your wedding band lighting: stage lights, dance-floor effects, venue uplighting and the questions to ask about equipment, space and smoke or haze.",excerpt:"What lighting do you need for your wedding band? A practical guide to stage lights, dance-floor effects and checking what your booking includes.",category:"Wedding Planning",author:"Rhona Downie",authorType:"Person",publishedAt:"2017-05-08",readTime:"5 min read",heroImage:"/images/blog/jen-and-tom/band-and-dance-floor.webp",heroImageAlt:"A live wedding band and guests on a chequered dance floor beneath colourful stage lights and a star-lit marquee ceiling",heroImageWidth:2048,heroImageHeight:1365,heroImageFit:"contain",content:[{type:"paragraph",text:"Great wedding music deserves a setting that feels ready for a party. Lighting helps bring the band and dance floor together, but you do not need to know the name of every fixture to make a good plan. Start with three questions: what is included with your chosen band, what does your venue already provide, and what atmosphere would you like?"},{type:"paragraph",text:"Originally published on 8 May 2017; refreshed on 22 October 2026. The equipment discussed here is a guide to the options, not a promise that every band supplies the same lighting. Confirm your chosen act’s current package and any extras before booking."},{type:"heading",text:"Stage lighting and dance-floor lighting do different jobs"},{type:"paragraph",text:"Stage lighting helps guests see the musicians, while dance-floor lighting adds colour and movement around the party. Decorative venue lighting, such as uplighting around the room or a star-lit ceiling, contributes another layer. Check these separately: an attractive room does not necessarily mean the band or dance floor will be lit."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Tell us where the musicians and dancing will be positioned, especially if they are in different spaces. A photograph of the room and an indication of the available performance area can help everyone agree an appropriate setup."},{type:"heading",text:"Disco T-bars and compact lighting bars"},{type:"paragraph",text:"A lighting bar combines several fixtures on a stand, making it an option for adding colour around a compact performance area. Depending on the equipment, lights may be aimed towards the band or dance floor and may use automatic, sound-activated or controlled programmes. A sound-activated effect responds to sound; it is not a guarantee that every flash will follow the beat in the same way."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Ask what your band’s lighting actually covers rather than relying on the phrase “lighting included”. Is it mainly for the performers, mainly for dancing, or both? Does it suit the size and layout of your room? If you prefer a gentle colour wash to lots of movement, mention that when discussing the setup."},{type:"heading",text:"LED party effects and disco balls"},{type:"paragraph",text:"Our original article also featured an LED disco-ball effect. This is worth distinguishing from a traditional mirrored disco ball: equipment described as a “disco ball” can produce quite different looks. Ask for a photograph or a recent performance clip showing the proposed effect, rather than assuming a particular fixture will be supplied."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Think about how you want the evening to feel. You might prefer a softer look for your first dance and more colour for the party afterwards. Discuss that with the band, including any effects you would rather avoid."},{type:"image",src:"/images/blog/jen-and-tom/first-dance.webp",width:2048,height:1365,alt:"The bride and groom sharing their first dance in front of a live band, with pink light across the chequered dance floor",caption:"Jen and Tom’s 2017 wedding at The Old Hall Ely. Wedding-day photography: Natalja Safronova. This historic photograph illustrates the setting, not a current equipment package."},{type:"heading",text:"What does the venue already provide?"},{type:"paragraph",text:"Before adding extras, ask your venue about its existing room and dance-floor lighting, who operates it, and whether the settings can change during the evening. Also check where stands can go, what space needs to remain clear, and whether the band needs to coordinate setup with the venue’s team."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Our venue guide is a useful starting point for planning the music, but confirm current arrangements directly with your venue. Layouts and equipment can change between weddings.",links:[{text:"venue guide",href:"/venues/"}]},{type:"heading",text:"Check before planning smoke, haze or flashing effects"},{type:"paragraph",text:"If you are considering smoke, fog or haze, get the venue’s approval before arranging it and ask the supplier to agree its use with the venue team. Do not assume it is allowed just because you have seen it in another wedding video. Ask about any alarm-system or room restrictions, and flag any concerns about flashing effects or guest sensitivities in advance so alternatives can be discussed."},{type:"heading",text:"Your wedding lighting checklist"},{type:"paragraph",text:"Sound restrictions are another early planning question. Our guide to sound limiters at wedding venues explains what to ask before confirming your band.",links:[{text:"sound limiters at wedding venues",href:"/blog/sound-limiters-at-wedding-venues-what-you-need-to-know/"}]},{type:"paragraph",text:"Before confirming the booking, agree: the lighting included with the chosen act; the stage and dance-floor areas it will cover; any venue lighting you will use; setup space and access; the look you want for the first dance and party; any restricted effects; and the price of any agreed extras. Keep those details with your booking arrangements so the band, venue and any DJ are working to the same plan."},{type:"heading",text:"See a real wedding, then plan your own"},{type:"paragraph",text:"For an example of a band performing in a star-lit marquee, explore Jen and Tom’s wedding at The Old Hall Ely. Their celebration shows how the band, room and dance floor can come together, although today’s arrangements should always be checked separately.",links:[{text:"Jen and Tom’s wedding at The Old Hall Ely",href:"/blog/jen-and-toms-wedding/"}]},{type:"paragraph",text:"Ready to find your band? Browse our wedding bands and add your date and venue, or get in touch to discuss the music and lighting you have in mind. We can help you check the details with your chosen act.",links:[{text:"Browse our wedding bands",href:"/acts/"},{text:"get in touch",href:"/contact/"}]}]},e=(a,i)=>({type:"paragraph",text:a,...i?{links:i}:{}}),n=a=>({type:"heading",text:a}),t=(a,i)=>({text:a,href:i}),r=(a,i,h,g,c)=>({type:"image",src:`/images/blog/london-hotels/${a}.webp`,width:i,height:h,alt:g,caption:c}),l={"The Dilly, Piccadilly — formerly Le Méridien Piccadilly":r("le-meridien-piccadilly",1101,735,"Ornate gold-toned reception room with chandeliers, round dining tables and a decorated wedding stage at the former Le Méridien Piccadilly","Archive photograph of Le Méridien Piccadilly, now The Dilly. The decor and layout shown are historical."),"The Savoy":r("savoy",732,343,"Wedding reception tables with clear chairs and pastel flower arrangements in a pale blue and white room at The Savoy","Archive photograph supplied for The Savoy; not a confirmation of the room’s current layout."),"The Dorchester":r("dorchester",1200,600,"Wedding ceremony aisle between silver chairs, with white flowers, blue drapes and chandeliers at The Dorchester","Archive wedding ceremony photograph at The Dorchester."),"London Marriott Hotel Marble Arch":r("marriott-marble-arch",610,423,"Round wedding breakfast tables with white chair covers and tall green flower arrangements in the Marriott hotel’s chandelier-lit room","Archive photograph supplied for the Marriott entry in our original article."),"Sheraton Grand London Park Lane":r("sheraton-grand",1600,900,"Round dining tables and white-covered chairs facing the curtained stage in the Sheraton Grand’s Art Deco ballroom","Archive ballroom photograph at Sheraton Grand London Park Lane."),"The Ritz London":r("ritz",768,420,"Long formal dining table surrounded by red upholstered chairs in an ornate red-and-gold private room at The Ritz","Archive private-dining photograph at The Ritz London."),"The Waldorf Hilton, London":r("waldorf-hilton",772,768,"Wedding breakfast tables with green patterned chair covers beneath the Waldorf Hilton’s bright gridded glass ceiling","Archive wedding reception photograph at The Waldorf Hilton."),"The Landmark London":r("landmark",1600,1067,"Candlelit wedding tables with tall white flower arrangements, marble columns and chandeliers at The Landmark London","Archive wedding reception photograph at The Landmark London."),"Grand Sapphire, Croydon":r("grand-sapphire",638,447,"Wedding reception tables with gold chairs, chandeliers and pink ceiling lighting at Grand Sapphire","Archive wedding reception photograph at Grand Sapphire."),"Portman Square — formerly Radisson Blu, now Nobu Hotel London Portman Square":r("radisson-blu-portman-square",1280,960,"White-covered wedding tables and chairs beneath crystal chandeliers in the former Radisson Blu Portman Square ballroom","Historical Radisson Blu Portman Square photograph, before its redevelopment as Nobu. This does not show the current Nobu interior.")},m={slug:"sound-limiters-at-wedding-venues-what-you-need-to-know",title:"Sound Limiters at Wedding Venues: What You Need to Know",metaTitle:"Wedding Venue Sound Limiters: Questions to Ask | The Supreme Collective",metaDescription:"Choosing a wedding venue with a sound limiter? Understand the setup, questions to ask and possible band adaptations before confirming your live music.",excerpt:"Ask about sound restrictions before booking your venue: a practical guide to limiters, band setups and planning a great evening of live music.",category:"Wedding Planning",author:"Rhona Downie",authorType:"Person",publishedAt:"2025-04-04",readTime:"5 min read",heroImage:"/images/blog/jen-and-tom/five-piece-band.webp",heroImageWidth:2048,heroImageHeight:1365,heroImageFit:"contain",heroImageAlt:"Five-piece wedding band with two vocalists, guitar, bass and drums posing onstage beneath coloured lights",heroImageCaption:"Illustrative band photograph from Jen and Tom’s 2017 wedding, by Natalja Safronova. It does not show a sound limiter or indicate that this venue has one.",content:[e("Planning a wedding comes with a long list of decisions. If live music is important to you, ask your venue one question early: “Do you have a sound limiter?” Knowing the answer before you book gives your musicians time to assess the room, discuss any restrictions and recommend a suitable setup."),e("Originally published on 4 April 2025; restored and updated on 23 October 2026. We have refreshed the technical guidance: a decibel number alone cannot tell you whether a particular band will work in a room, and equipment changes are not a guarantee of compliance."),n("What is a sound limiter?"),e("A venue sound limiter monitors or controls sound against a configured limit. Some systems reduce the audio signal; others can interrupt the power supplied to the music equipment when their trigger conditions are met. Ask which system is installed and what it controls. These are different arrangements, so “we have a limiter” is only the start of the conversation."),e("For examples of those different approaches, manufacturer Formula Sound describes both cut-off limiters and automatic volume-control systems.",[t("Formula Sound","https://formula-sound.co.uk/system-protection-noise-control/")]),n("Why do venues use them?"),e("Ask the venue team to explain its noise requirements and how they apply to your wedding. Get the rules for the specific room, performance area and finish time in writing, and pass them to your band before you confirm the entertainment. Do not assume that arrangements at a different wedding or another room in the same building will apply to yours."),n("Why the decibel limit is not the whole answer"),e("Ask for the limiter make and model, its threshold, the measurement weighting and the microphone position. Systems can use different weighting settings and internal or external microphones. Your band or sound engineer needs that context, not just a number such as “95 dB”, to assess the proposed setup."),e("The position of the musicians, speakers and monitoring equipment should be discussed with the venue team. Ask how long the level can exceed the threshold before the system responds, whether settings change during the evening, and who deals with a trip or reset. Do not move, cover or bypass monitoring equipment."),n("What can a band do about it?"),e("Depending on the musicians, repertoire and venue requirements, options to discuss include an electronic drum kit, a quieter acoustic-drum approach, fewer onstage amplifiers, direct connections to a suitable PA, and in-ear monitoring instead of stage wedges. These are possibilities to agree with the chosen act, not equipment every band automatically supplies."),e("In-ear monitors can reduce the need for monitor speakers, but they do not make acoustic drums, brass, vocals or the audience silent. Likewise, connecting instruments directly to a PA does not remove the need to control the sound coming out of that PA. Ask the band what adaptations are realistic without losing the style of performance you want."),n("Questions to ask before booking"),{type:"image",src:"/images/blog/jen-and-tom/band-and-dance-floor.webp",width:2048,height:1365,alt:"Wedding guests dancing in front of the live band beneath a star-lit marquee ceiling",caption:"Planning the band and dance floor together. Illustrative 2017 photograph by Natalja Safronova; no limiter status is implied for the pictured venue."},e("Does the venue have a limiter, and what type is it? What is the threshold and measurement setup? Must the band use the venue’s PA or approved technician? What equipment and stage positions are permitted? Are there restrictions on drums or brass? When must live music and recorded music finish? Can the band soundcheck before guests enter, and who is responsible for operating the system?"),n("Choose the venue and band together"),e("Think about the celebration you want: a relaxed acoustic set, a compact party band or a larger lineup with brass. Share that vision and the venue’s technical requirements with us early. “We have had bands before” is useful background, but it is not confirmation that every lineup will suit the same conditions."),e("Browse our venue guide, explore wedding bands, or get in touch with your venue details so we can discuss the right musical setup. Lighting has its own planning questions too; our wedding band lighting guide explains what to check.",[t("venue guide","/venues/"),t("wedding bands","/acts/"),t("get in touch","/contact/"),t("wedding band lighting guide","/blog/essential-stage-lighting-for-your-wedding-band/")])]},s=(a,i,h)=>[n(a),...l[a]?[l[a]]:[],e(i),e("Check current venue information and arrangements directly with the hotel.",[t("current venue information",h)])],f={slug:"londons-swankiest-hotels-for-your-wedding",title:"London’s Swankiest Hotels for Your Wedding",metaTitle:"Luxury London Wedding Hotels & Live Music Planning | The Supreme Collective",metaDescription:"Revisit our London wedding hotel shortlist, with updated hotel names, official venue links and practical questions for planning your live band.",excerpt:"Our original London hotel shortlist, revisited with current names and the live-music questions to ask before booking your wedding venue.",category:"Wedding Venues",author:"Rhona Downie",authorType:"Person",publishedAt:"2017-06-11",readTime:"6 min read",heroImage:"/images/blog/london-hotels/dorchester.webp",heroImageWidth:1200,heroImageHeight:600,heroImageFit:"contain",heroImageAlt:"Wedding ceremony setup at The Dorchester with a white-flower-lined aisle, silver chairs and blue-and-gold drapes",heroImageCaption:"Archive wedding photograph at The Dorchester. The venue photographs in this article show historical settings, not guaranteed current layouts.",content:[e("A hotel wedding can bring the ceremony, wedding breakfast, evening party and somewhere to stay together in one place. From a band’s perspective, the most useful conversations are practical ones: where we will perform, how equipment gets into the room, when soundcheck can happen and how the evening moves from dinner to dancing."),e("This shortlist first appeared in June 2017, drawing on hotels and venues we had performed at. Restored on 23 October 2026, it keeps those original choices while noting two changed hotel names and linking to current venue information. Our past performance history does not imply a current partnership or preferred-supplier status. Capacities, packages, staffing, access and music policies must be checked directly; the old article’s figures and complimentary-package promises have not been carried forward."),...s("The Dilly, Piccadilly — formerly Le Méridien Piccadilly","Our original entry focused on the Oak Room, with its oak panelling and chandeliers. The property is now known as The Dilly. If you are considering a wedding here, ask which rooms are available for your date and how the chosen room will accommodate both the band and dance floor.","https://www.thedillylondon.com/events/weddings"),e("The former owner’s account confirms the change from Le Méridien to The Dilly.",[t("former owner’s account","https://www.archerhotelcapital.com/archer-completes-sale-of-the-dilly-hotel/")]),...s("The Savoy","The Lancaster Ballroom was a highlight of our original list, particularly for dancing and its stage. The Savoy still describes a stage in its current ballroom information. Ask the events team about the performance position, the equipment route into the room and the layout once dinner service gives way to the evening party.","https://www.thesavoylondon.com/events-and-celebrations/lancaster-ballroom"),...s("The Dorchester","The original article chose The Dorchester for its luxurious wedding setting. For your own celebration, establish the room and guest layout first, then discuss the band’s footprint, preparation space, setup schedule and any technical requirements with the hotel and musicians together.","https://www.dorchestercollection.com/london/the-dorchester"),...s("London Marriott Hotel Marble Arch","Our original entry highlighted this Marble Arch hotel as a base for a London celebration close to Hyde Park and the West End. Ask for the current wedding-room options and confirm how much space remains for musicians, speakers and dancing after tables are in place.","https://www.marriott.com/en-us/hotels/lonma-london-marriott-hotel-marble-arch/events/"),...s("Sheraton Grand London Park Lane","The Art Deco ballroom, separate entrance and built-in stage made this a memorable choice in the original shortlist. Those are useful starting points for the music plan, but agree the current access arrangements, technical provision and room layout rather than assuming every wedding uses the same setup.","https://www.marriott.com/en-gb/hotels/event-planning/wedding-planning/longr-sheraton-grand-london-park-lane/"),...s("The Ritz London","Our original feature centred on William Kent House and its elegant private rooms. If your day uses more than one space, discuss where the music belongs at each stage, whether equipment needs to move, and whether an intimate musical setup or an evening party lineup best suits the booked room.","https://www.theritzlondon.com/"),...s("The Waldorf Hilton, London","Palm Court and the Adelphi Suite featured in the original article and remain named spaces on the hotel’s current events page. Ask how your chosen rooms will be used through the day, whether there is a room change or turnaround, and when the band can set up without interrupting the celebration.","https://www.hilton.com/en/hotels/lonwahi-the-waldorf-hilton-london/events/"),...s("The Landmark London","Our original shortlist highlighted the hotel’s wedding planning and different banqueting spaces. For live music, start with the specific room and seating plan: a ballroom and a smaller reception space can call for quite different band and PA arrangements.","https://www.landmarklondon.co.uk/meetings-events/"),...s("Grand Sapphire, Croydon","Grand Sapphire brought a larger celebration option to the original list. Its current website identifies it as a wedding, event and hotel venue in Croydon. Discuss the booked suite, performance area and sound coverage with the team rather than choosing a band setup from the guest count alone.","https://grandsapphire.co.uk/"),...s("Portman Square — formerly Radisson Blu, now Nobu Hotel London Portman Square","The original article listed Radisson Blu Portman Square. That property was subsequently redeveloped as Nobu Hotel London Portman Square; the project architect documents the transformation. Treat it as a changed venue rather than relying on the old room descriptions or capacity figures, and ask the current events team about your wedding plans.","https://www.nobuhotels.com/london-portman/explore/faq/"),e("The project architect’s account explains the former Radisson Blu’s renovation into Nobu.",[t("project architect’s account","https://bsbgroup.com/blog/nobu-hotel-london-portman-square-wins-prestigious-hotel-design-award")]),n("Planning the music for your London hotel wedding"),e("Before confirming your entertainment, agree the available stage space, loading access, setup and soundcheck times, sound restrictions, power provision, changing space and supplier meals. These should be confirmed for your booking; they are not automatic inclusions simply because a venue is a hotel."),e("Explore wedding bands in London, browse our venue guide, and read our sound-limiters guide before finalising the music. Send us your date and venue to discuss a lineup that fits your celebration.",[t("wedding bands in London","/wedding-bands-london/"),t("venue guide","/venues/?county=London"),t("sound-limiters guide","/blog/sound-limiters-at-wedding-venues-what-you-need-to-know/"),t("date and venue","/acts/")])]},w={slug:"get-to-know-phoebe",title:"Get to Know Phoebe",metaTitle:"Get to Know Phoebe: A Singer’s Wedding Diary | The Supreme Collective",metaDescription:"Meet singer Phoebe through our 2017 archive interview and her account of performing with Claire at Joe and Kirsten’s wedding at Eastington Park.",excerpt:"A look back at Phoebe’s favourite music and her 2017 diary of performing at Joe and Kirsten’s wedding at Eastington Park.",category:"Behind the Music",author:"Rhona Downie",authorType:"Person",publishedAt:"2017-05-25",readTime:"4 min read",heroImage:"/images/blog/phoebe/phoebe-performing.webp",heroImageWidth:1500,heroImageHeight:1e3,heroImageFit:"contain",heroImageAlt:"Phoebe singing into a handheld microphone with one arm raised, alongside another vocalist under red stage lighting",heroImageCaption:"Phoebe performing with another vocalist, from the original article’s archive photographs.",content:[e("From our 2017 archive, restored on 23 October 2026. This interview and wedding diary describe Phoebe’s work and preferences at the time; they are not a statement of her current representation, location or availability. The diary below is Phoebe’s account, lightly edited for spelling and readability."),e("Our original introduction described Phoebe as an accomplished singer-songwriter with classic pop tones and an infectious love of performing. It recorded her work in the UK and Los Angeles, writing and recording with artists including Pharrell Williams, and her experience as an X Factor finalist mentored by Simon Cowell. Those biographical details are retained here as part of the original 2017 account, rather than a newly checked current biography."),n("Phoebe’s favourites — as shared in 2017"),e("Food: fish and chips. Colour: baby blue. Song at the time: “Only Love Can Hurt Like This” by Paloma Faith. Artists: Aretha Franklin and Beyoncé. Sports: running and badminton. Drinks: apple juice and red wine. In her downtime, Phoebe enjoyed shopping in central London and exploring the museums."),n("Performances from the original article"),e("The original page linked to Phoebe Brown singing “(You Make Me Feel Like) A Natural Woman” at Ronnie Scott’s and an acoustic performance of “FourFiveSeconds”. Watch them on YouTube; these are external archive links and availability may change.",[t("“(You Make Me Feel Like) A Natural Woman” at Ronnie Scott’s","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tlrtEawdygU"),t("an acoustic performance of “FourFiveSeconds”","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2CvTLuPDdQw")]),n("Diary entry, 25 May 2017: Joe and Kirsten’s wedding"),e("Last weekend we performed and partied away on stage with the fabulous Joe and Kirsten."),e("Joe and Kirsten chose me and Claire to sing at their wedding, which worked out perfectly as we lived fairly close to each other. We set off from London to Eastington Park, a beautiful manor house near Bristol and Bath."),e("It took us about two hours to drive there and, of course, we stopped for coffee on the way! We usually got to gigs about four or five hours before we were meant to go on stage because we liked to soundcheck before the guests arrived. We also had to do our hair and makeup, to perfection of course!"),e("The venue itself was truly beautiful and not only oozed elegance but had a really homely vibe. We were in our changing room and exploring the grounds for about three hours before we were called to soundcheck. In this time, guests were finishing up their food, which looked amazing, and cutting the cake."),e("We hopped onto the stage and completed our soundcheck, and then all the guests came in. They immediately joined in the fun and started dancing with us. We usually opened with the crowd-pleasing “Respect” by Aretha Franklin, which always got everyone going! For this gig, the songs chosen by Joe and Kirsten were absolutely excellent. They chose a mix of pop and Motown, which I especially love. One of my favourites that we performed was “Uptown Funk”."),e("Claire and I partied on stage and even went into the audience with the guests. Claire is excellent at getting the party going, and we really do have fun on stage together. I always love to sing with Claire; we have a really good connection and chemistry on stage."),e("After our party band performance came to an end, we naturally did an encore, and still the guests were hoping for more. We had to call it a night at midnight under the venue arrangements for that wedding. The band packed up and we headed home at about 1am."),e("Overall, the gig was awesome, and I wish Joe and Kirsten all the happiness in the world! Phoebe x"),e("The timings and venue arrangements above relate to that 2017 wedding, not Eastington Park’s current policy or a standard arrival time for every band. Confirm the schedule for your own booking separately."),n("Planning your own live music?"),e("Explore our current bands, browse the venue guide or get in touch about your celebration. Individual musician availability must be confirmed for your date.",[t("current bands","/acts/"),t("venue guide","/venues/"),t("get in touch","/contact/")])]},y=[m,f,w],b=[{slug:"5-ways-live-music-transforms-an-event",title:"5 Ways Live Music Transforms an Event",metaTitle:"5 Ways Live Music Transforms an Event | Wedding Entertainment Ideas",metaDescription:"Discover 5 ways live music transforms weddings and events, from atmosphere and emotion to guest energy and unforgettable dance floor moments.",excerpt:"From atmosphere and emotion to guest energy and unforgettable moments, here are 5 ways live music can completely transform a wedding or event.",category:"Wedding Entertainment",author:"The Supreme Collective",publishedAt:"2026-03-11",readTime:"6 min read",heroImage:"/images/blog/live-music-transforms-event-hero.jpg",heroImageAlt:"Wedding guests celebrating together beneath red heart-shaped balloons",content:[{type:"paragraph",text:"When people think about booking wedding entertainment, they often picture one thing first: a packed dance floor."},{type:"paragraph",text:"And yes, that is a huge part of it."},{type:"paragraph",text:"There is nothing quite like seeing a room come alive, watching guests let go, and hearing afterwards that people danced so hard their feet were aching for days. We are always so grateful to receive messages like that after an event, because it reminds us just how powerful live music can be. Great music does not just fill a room with sound. It creates moments that stay with people for years."},{type:"paragraph",text:"But the truth is, live music does much more than get people dancing."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Whether you are planning a wedding, a private party or a corporate celebration, live music changes the entire feel of an occasion. It transforms the atmosphere, lifts guest energy, creates emotional highlights and makes the whole experience feel more immersive and memorable."},{type:"paragraph",text:"As we head into the busier half of the year, we have some incredible events coming up. Some clients are using the band to build the perfect atmosphere before handing over to a DJ later in the evening. Others are planning rustic, festival-style weddings with acoustic performances woven into the day. Later in the year, corporate summer parties and Christmas events will bring another kind of energy altogether."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Every one of them is a reminder that live music does not just entertain people. It moves them."},{type:"heading",text:"1. It sets the atmosphere from the very first moment"},{type:"paragraph",text:"One of the most powerful things about live music is how quickly it can shape the mood of an event."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Before the dance floor is even open, the music is already doing its job. A mellow acoustic set can make a drinks reception feel warm and welcoming. A soulful live performance can make dinner feel elegant and intimate. A carefully paced evening set can take guests from relaxed conversation to full celebration in a way that feels completely natural."},{type:"paragraph",text:"That is one of the biggest advantages of live music for wedding receptions and private events. Live musicians can adapt in real time. They are not just pressing play. They are reading the room, responding to the atmosphere and helping the event unfold with the right feeling at the right moment."},{type:"paragraph",text:"For some of our upcoming weddings, the band is being used as a beautifully judged build-up to the later party. The first set is mellow and stylish, the second set lifts into something more upbeat, and the final set gets everyone onto the dance floor before handing over to the DJ. It is a brilliant example of how a wedding band and DJ can work together to create a seamless evening."},{type:"heading",text:"2. It creates emotional moments people actually remember"},{type:"paragraph",text:"Live music has a way of making moments land more deeply."},{type:"paragraph",text:"It could be the emotion of hearing a favourite song performed live. It could be the warmth of an acoustic set during an outdoor drinks reception. It could be the goosebumps that come from an incredible vocalist delivering a song at exactly the right point in the evening."},{type:"paragraph",text:"There is something human, immediate and personal about live performance that recorded music cannot quite replicate. It feels more present. More real. More connected."},{type:"paragraph",text:"That is why live music works so beautifully across so many different parts of a celebration. It is not only about the evening party. It can add real value to the entire event, from ceremony music and drinks receptions to the final dance floor set."},{type:"paragraph",text:"For couples searching for live music for wedding reception ideas, this is often the real magic. Live music does not just sound good. It makes the important moments feel bigger."},{type:"image",src:"/images/blog/emotional-moment.jpg",alt:"Couple sharing an emotional wedding dance surrounded by guests and sparklers"},{type:"heading",text:"3. It turns guests from spectators into participants"},{type:"paragraph",text:"A great live band does not just perform at people. It draws them in."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Guests respond differently to live music because it feels interactive and alive. They sing along more. They dance sooner. They cheer louder. They connect more easily. Even guests who thought they would stay seated often find themselves getting up for one song and then staying on the dance floor far longer than they expected."},{type:"paragraph",text:"This is where live music can completely transform guest energy."},{type:"paragraph",text:"At some events, that interaction becomes part of the entertainment itself. Think roaming acoustic musicians performing among guests in a festival-style setting. A saxophonist stepping onto the dance floor. A vocalist using a wireless mic to join the crowd. Guests having an impromptu dance-off with the band."},{type:"paragraph",text:"These are the kinds of moments people remember because they feel spontaneous, exciting and genuinely unique."},{type:"image",src:"/images/blog/audience-interaction.jpg",alt:"Singer interacting with a bride during a live performance"},{type:"paragraph",text:"If you are looking for bands to play at weddings or private events, this is one of the biggest advantages of choosing a strong live act. The entertainment becomes part of the experience, not just background noise."},{type:"heading",text:"4. It gives the event more personality and style"},{type:"paragraph",text:"No two events are the same, and the entertainment should reflect that."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Some couples want elegant, polished live music throughout the day. Some want a high-energy luxury wedding band feel for the evening. Others want something more relaxed and unexpected, such as acoustic roaming sets during the afternoon. Some are looking for a funk and soul wedding band to bring warmth and groove. Others want a Motown wedding band vibe that gets every generation onto the dance floor."},{type:"paragraph",text:"That is the beauty of live music. It can be tailored to the personality of the event."},{type:"paragraph",text:"We have weddings coming up where the live band is there to create a sophisticated build before a DJ-led late-night finish. We have rustic, festival-style celebrations where the music moves with the guests and creates surprise moments throughout the day. Later in the year, we will also be performing at corporate summer parties and Christmas events where the music helps people relax, celebrate and connect outside of the usual work setting."},{type:"paragraph",text:"Whether you are looking for wedding musicians in London, a live band for a wedding in the UK, or a versatile party band for hire, live music brings personality in a way that makes the event feel far more bespoke."},{type:"image",src:"/images/blog/roaming-band.jpg",alt:"Roaming live musicians performing outdoors at an event"},{type:"heading",text:"5. It creates memories guests talk about long after the event"},{type:"paragraph",text:"The best events are not just the ones that look beautiful on the day. They are the ones people keep talking about afterwards."},{type:"paragraph",text:"And time and again, live music becomes one of the most talked-about parts of an event. Guests remember how it felt when the band kicked in. They remember the moment the dance floor filled. They remember the singer’s voice, the saxophone solo, the crowd interaction and the song that everyone shouted back word for word."},{type:"image",src:"/images/blog/sax-in-crowd.jpg",alt:"Saxophonist performing among guests on a packed dance floor"},{type:"paragraph",text:"Those are the moments that last."},{type:"paragraph",text:"For us, that is one of the greatest privileges of what we do. We love music, and we perform for people who love music. We get to help create joy, connection, release, celebration and nostalgia, sometimes all in the same night. We are there for the emotional journey of the event, and we want people to leave feeling on top of the world."},{type:"paragraph",text:"That is why booking live music is about much more than hiring a band."},{type:"paragraph",text:"It is about creating an atmosphere your guests can feel. It is about making the celebration more immersive, more energetic and more memorable. It is about turning a great event into an unforgettable one."},{type:"heading",text:"Final thoughts: why live music makes such a difference"},{type:"paragraph",text:"If you are considering entertainment hire for weddings, planning your evening party, or exploring ideas for live music for wedding receptions, a great live band is one of the most powerful ways to elevate the experience."},{type:"paragraph",text:"From atmosphere and emotion to energy and interaction, live music transforms the feel of an event in a way few other entertainment options can."},{type:"paragraph",text:"A brilliant live band does not just play songs. It creates moments. And those moments are often the ones your guests remember most."}]},...y,p,u];export{b};
